// art.ron — the registry. ONE line per asset, ONE line to swap it (init.md §7).
//
// The code never asks for a file, always for a logical name. Swapping an asset means:
// change `source:` here — not touching Rust.
//
//   source: Primitive              ⇒ the placeholder path built from Bevy primitives.
//   source: Gltf("3d/glb/<file>.glb")   ⇒ that file, relative to `assets/`.
//
// Both paths have to run at any time and carry the same size, hit zone and scale.
// **The game runs with not a single .glb in the repository** — that is what `Primitive`
// is for, and it is a named variant and not a missing field so a typo crashes at load
// instead of turning into an empty screen (docs/conventions.md §4).
//
// `animations:` maps a GAME STATE to the name of the clip inside the .glb. An empty map
// means "this model animates nothing"; a name that is not in the file is a loud warning at
// load and that state simply having no clip — never a silent substitute.
//
// ===================================================================================
// THE ASSET DROP — read this before adding a row (2026-08-18)
// ===================================================================================
//
// `assets/3d/glb/` holds **278 .glb (26 MB)** and `assets/texturen/` the **17 atlases
// (16 MB)** they paint themselves with. Both are TRACKED on purpose (`.gitignore` carries an
// explicit `!assets/3d/glb/**`), because a registry that names a file the repository does not
// have is an empty screen with no error — which is the one failure this whole file exists to
// prevent.
//
// ⚠️ **The folder `assets/texturen/` MUST NOT BE RENAMED.** Every .glb references its atlas
// from the inside, by the relative URI `../../texturen/TEX-*.png`, which resolves out of
// `assets/3d/glb/`. Rename the folder and all 278 models lose their textures at once — with a
// warning, not an error. The German name is the pack's own internal contract and is therefore
// one of the places CLAUDE.md rule 2 does not reach.
//
// The pack came with its own `data/*.ron` (the old German scaffolding, `skala.ron` and
// friends). Those were **deliberately not copied**: `assets/data/*.ron` here is newer and is
// the authority. Nothing in this repository reads anything out of the drop except the .glb
// files themselves.
//
// ## What every model in the drop carries, measured over all 278 files
//
//   `hit.min` / `hit.max`   278 of 278 — a machine-readable bounding box, as two empties.
//                           ⚠️ It is a CORNER PAIR, not an ordered AABB: on all 278 files
//                           `hit.max.z < hit.min.z` (the Blender +Y-forward -> glTF
//                           -Z-forward flip). Whoever consumes it takes a componentwise
//                           min/max, never `max - min`. The one consumer today is
//                           `render::model::fit_to_class`, and it takes `.abs()`.
//   `cortex`                45 of 278 — where the titan dies. THE anchor the game reads.
//   `hand.l` / `hand.r`      106 · `eye` 92 · `hook.l` / `hook.r` 63 files.
//   `hook.<german>`         439 further empties across 144 files — roof eaves
//                           (`hook.traufe`), ridges (`hook.first`), crowns (`hook.krone`),
//                           the wall's cornice ladder (`hook.gesims_15..105`).
//                           **The loader keeps them since 2026-08-18**: `hook.` is an OPEN
//                           family in `shared::anchors::is_anchor_name`, because the 565
//                           hook empties carry **212 distinct names and 130 of those appear
//                           in exactly one file** — a whitelist is structurally impossible.
//                           The eight rig landmarks stay a closed list, so a Blender typo
//                           still reads as *missing* and not as *new*.
//                           ⚠️ **Zero of the 439 reach the loader in the shipped game**, and
//                           that is not this file's doing: they all sit in the architecture
//                           kit, and no world block carries a `ModelName` yet (see the
//                           `house_*` rows). Nothing consumes a per-model anchor either —
//                           `vector::aim` raycasts avian colliders and never reads
//                           `ModelAnchors`; the only consumer of any anchor in the tree is
//                           `titan::rig::cortex_from_the_model`, and it reads `cortex` alone.
//
// ## `animations: {}` is not laziness — the drop has ZERO clips
//
// All 278 files carry an empty glTF `animations` array; the two rigs
// (`a-001-basis-rig-vanguard`, `a-040-titan-basis-rig`) are armatures without a single
// authored clip. **Do not invent a clip name here.** A name that is not in the file is a loud
// warning at load *and* brings the cuboid rig back on screen the moment the game enters that
// state (`PrimitiveFallback`, `src/render/model.rs`) — you would trade a model for a
// placeholder. `{}` is the only honest value until somebody authors a clip, and it stays `{}`
// on the BOUND row below as well: it is the proof of that rule, not an exception to it.
// Re-derived twice already — this paragraph is the answer, do not measure it a third time, and
// `tests/render.rs::f030_a_bound_row_names_no_clip_because_its_file_carries_none` reads the
// file's own JSON chunk so the day somebody authors a walk cycle the map may be filled in.
//
// ## `scale:` stays 1.0, and that is enforced by a test
//
// The drop is authored in the game's exact metres — the door leaf measures 2.100 m against
// `scale.ron reference.door_height_m 2.1`, the three half-timbered houses 4.50 / 8.00 / 11.50
// against `heights_m`, the wall segments 120.00 against `wall.height_m`. So 1.0 is not a
// default here, it is the measurement. Per-entity sizing is not this field's job either:
// `render::model` scales a model to the height `scale.ron` gives the ENTITY, out of the
// model's own `hit.min`/`hit.max`. That is what lets `titan_husk` dress a 10 m husk and a
// 4.2 m scuttler out of one file, the way `titan.ron` already asks it to.
// `tests/render.rs::f030_every_configured_row_is_drawn_at_the_scale_it_was_authored_in` holds
// the 1.0 down.
//
// ## ONE of the ten rows ships BOUND — the two rules that decide it
//
// 1. **A registry row is only worth wiring when something spawns it.** Exactly one system in
//    the whole tree inserts a `ModelName`: `render::model::name_the_titans_model`, driven by
//    `titan.ron`. So `titan_husk` and `titan_large` are the only two names any entity in the
//    running game asks for. Pointing any other row at a file would load glTF and put nothing
//    on screen — and it would make this file claim something the game does not do.
// 2. **A binding that takes a 🟧 test red is a wrong binding, not a red test.** That is what
//    holds `titan_large` back today, measured and written out at the row itself.
//
// So: `titan_husk` is bound and dresses **five of the eight titan kinds** (husk, errant,
// chorus, scuttler, weaver); the other nine rows are `Primitive`. **What each unbound row is
// waiting for, and the file that would dress it, stands next to it**, so the next session does
// not re-derive it.
(
    models: {
        // ---- the district's titans · `titan_husk` is BOUND and on screen ----
        //
        // The only two logical names any entity in the running game asks for, both out of
        // `titan.ron: kinds.*.model`. One file per body size serves several kinds, because
        // `render::model::fit_to_class` brings a model to the CORTEX height `scale.ron` names
        // for the kind wearing it, out of the model's own `hit.min`/`hit.max` — so a row is a
        // body type, not a height.
        //
        //   titan_husk  -> 3d/glb/a-042-koerpertyp-a-hager-mittel.glb
        //     worn by husk, errant, chorus (medium 10.0 / cortex 8.9) AND scuttler, weaver
        //     (small 4.2 / cortex 3.7). Authored 10.0566 m tall, cortex at 8.9000 m.
        //     Fitted: medium scale 1.0000 -> stands 10.057 m (0.057 out);
        //             small  scale 0.4157 -> stands  4.181 m (0.019 out).
        //   titan_large -> 3d/glb/a-042-koerpertyp-a-hager-gross.glb — picked, measured, and
        //     NOT bound. The blocker stands at the row itself, below.
        //     `hager` (gaunt) is the body titan.ron asks for — the warden's cover only works
        //     with arm length. The drop has no 21 m titan body of its own; its only 21 m model
        //     is `a-073-colossus-form`, and that one carries hook.l/hook.r, i.e. it is a
        //     PLAYER form and not an enemy.
        //
        // ✅ **The blocker that held these two back until 2026-08-18 is gone.** It was the
        // nape: the drop authors its `cortex` empty on the SKIN of the neck (0.139 m behind
        // the neck axis on the medium body, 0.194 m on the large one), and `titan::rig` builds
        // the kill zone at `head_m * 0.5` = 0.55 m behind it, which is the whole of Q-030 —
        // "the nape is cut from behind, never from the front". Taking the anchor's depth
        // literally made a husk cuttable from the FRONT (measured: blade -0.066 m past the
        // cortex on a front pass).
        // `titan::rig::cortex_in_head_from_model` now CLAMPS: `local.z.max(head_m * 0.5)`.
        // **The model decides the Cortex's height and its side; the rig decides the minimum
        // depth.** A model that puts its nape further BACK is still believed — that direction
        // can only sharpen the approach angle. The drop does not even agree with itself here
        // (`a-040-titan-basis-rig` and `a-046-cortex-mesh` both put the same anchor at
        // -0.450 m, the centre of the amber blob), so there was no single "what the model
        // says" to obey. Proven by `tests/titan.rs::f030_a_bound_model_cannot_drag_the_nape_
        // round_to_the_front` on a bound husk and a bound warden, front pass and rear pass.
        //
        // The renderer also turns the drop's frame into the game's — the pack faces +Z, the
        // game faces -Z, and unturned an aggroed husk shows the player its back
        // (`render::model::MODEL_FACES`).
        "titan_husk":     (source: Gltf("3d/glb/a-042-koerpertyp-a-hager-mittel.glb"), scale: 1.0, attribution: None, animations: {}),

        // 🔴 **`titan_large` STAYS `Primitive`, and it is not a size problem — it is 1.4 cm of x.**
        //
        // Binding it takes `tests/titan.rs::q031_the_nape_survives_a_titan_who_tracks_you` red:
        //   `warden: the pass at 0.2 m of air lands again (blade -0.020 m)`
        // Q-031 pinned that a `large` titan who TURNS while you cross eats the pass at 0.20 m of
        // air and leaves only 0.15 m. With the model bound the 0.20 m pass lands again — the
        // warden gets easier, silently, out of an art file.
        //
        // **What actually differs, measured on a spawned warden with the row bound (2026-08-18):**
        //   `the model's "cortex" empty moves the kill zone from 12.50 m to 12.50 m above the
        //    feet, and its depth of 0.19 m was held back to the rig's 0.77 m`
        // — the HEIGHT is identical (fit_to_class fits by the cortex, so it lands on 12.50 m
        // exactly) and the DEPTH is identical (the clamp holds it at `head_m * 0.5` = 0.77 m).
        // The one component left is x: `titan::rig` builds the computed Cortex at x = 0.0
        // (rig.rs:184) and the model authors its `cortex` empty at x = +0.0139, which is
        // -0.01394 after `MODEL_FACES` turns the frame. **1.39 cm sideways is the whole
        // difference, and it flips a pinned measurement** — which says Q-031's 0.20 m margin was
        // about a centimetre wide, not that the model is wrong.
        //
        // The drop's x is authoring noise, not anatomy: the same body type carries +0.010 on the
        // medium and +0.0139 on the large, and a nape is on the centre line. So the likely fix
        // is one line in **`src/titan/rig.rs::cortex_in_head_from_model`** — take the model's
        // HEIGHT (and the depth, when it is deeper) and leave x at the rig's 0.0, exactly as the
        // depth is already clamped. That is somebody else's file, so this row waits instead of
        // the test being edited.
        //
        // **The repro costs no rebuild — this file is data:**
        //   sed -i 's|"titan_large":    (source: Primitive|"titan_large":    (source: Gltf("3d/glb/a-042-koerpertyp-a-hager-gross.glb")|' assets/data/art.ron
        //   cargo test --test titan 2>&1 | grep -E '^test result'
        //
        // Everything else about it is ready and measured, so the flip is one line the day the
        // rig decides: worn by warden, lurker (large 14.0 / cortex 12.5) AND bellower (huge
        // 21.0 / cortex 18.7); authored 14.0722 m tall with its cortex at 12.4600 m; fitted it
        // stands 14.117 m (0.117 out) and 21.120 m (0.120 out) — both inside
        // `cortex_tolerance_m` (0.15), and the two tightest numbers in this file.
        // ⚠️ `bellower` cannot be spawned at all today: scale.ron `max_spawnable_class` caps at
        // "large".
        "titan_large":    (source: Primitive, scale: 1.0, attribution: None, animations: {}),

        // ---- UNBOUND · named, but nothing in the game carries the name yet ----
        // Each line names the file that WOULD dress it and the exact blocker. Until that is
        // cleared the row stays `Primitive`: a `Gltf` here costs the asset load and renders
        // nothing.

        // WOULD BE: 3d/glb/a-136-npc-vanguard.glb (1.814 m against scale.ron's 1.8, carries
        // hook.l/hook.r/hand.l/hand.r/eye — the match is ready and measured).
        // BLOCKER: no system puts a `ModelName` on the player body, and one cannot simply be
        // added — the camera is a CHILD of the player at eye height, so a body model on the
        // LOCAL player is seen from inside. This needs a first-person/third-person decision
        // (docs/QUESTIONS.md) before it needs a row. A remote player would already work.
        "vanguard":       (source: Primitive, scale: 1.0, attribution: None, animations: {}),

        // WOULD BE: 3d/glb/a-020-vector-gear-stufe-1.glb (tiers 2 and 3 exist as -stufe-2 /
        // -stufe-3; its hook.l/hook.r sit on the same three decimals as the body's, so it
        // needs no mount offset at all).
        // BLOCKER: nothing spawns a gear entity. The gear is state on the player today, not a
        // thing in the world.
        "vector_gear":    (source: Primitive, scale: 1.0, attribution: None, animations: {}),

        // WOULD BE: 3d/glb/a-024-klingen-paar-neu.glb (a PAIR, authored already in hand
        // position, 2.08 m along Z, matching the body's hand.l/hand.r; -gebrochen is the
        // broken pair). There is no single-blade file in the drop.
        // BLOCKER: no blade entity in the player's hands, and — being a pair — it hangs off
        // the same first/third-person decision as `vanguard`.
        "blade":          (source: Primitive, scale: 1.0, attribution: None, animations: {}),

        // ---- the three half-timbered houses: the pack is exact, the wiring is one hop short
        //
        // `world::map` now PLANS a model name (`BlockPlan.model`, `world::map::dress_for`) and
        // `dress_for` refuses any name whose row here is not `Gltf(...)` — so these three rows
        // are the switch for the whole district. Measured with the switch on: 602 of 937
        // houses dressed, 5155 -> 3393 blocks (-34 %), footprint moves a median 1.42 m.
        //
        // ✅ UNBLOCKED 2026-08-19, and the rows are switched **on**. The blocker was that
        // **`BlockPlan::spawn` did not insert the `ModelName`** — the type lived in
        // `src/render/model.rs` and `world` has no allow-list edge to `render`. It now lives
        // in `src/shared/anchors.rs` beside `ModelAnchors`, for the reason that folder
        // exists, and a planned block carries its name into the world. Switching them back to
        // `Primitive` is still one line each and still costs no code.
        //
        // The heights are exact against `scale.ron: architecture.heights_m` under the same
        // key, and `tests/world.rs::f003_the_dressing_catalogue_is_what_the_glb_files_really_
        // measure` parses the three files and holds all eleven numbers down.
        //
        // ⚠️ `house_small` additionally can never be worn in Ashgate: the district's
        // `min_height_m` is 6.5 and the model is authored at 4.50, so its smallest fit is
        // 9.5 x 12.0 m — outside every slot. That is the height band, not the pack.
        // 3d/glb/a-083-fachwerkhaus-klein.glb — 6.56 x 4.50 x 8.32 m.
        "house_small":    (source: Gltf("3d/glb/a-083-fachwerkhaus-klein.glb"), scale: 1.0, attribution: None, animations: {}),
        // 3d/glb/a-083-fachwerkhaus-stadthaus.glb — 9.10 x 8.00 x 7.90 m.
        "house_town":     (source: Gltf("3d/glb/a-083-fachwerkhaus-stadthaus.glb"), scale: 1.0, attribution: None, animations: {}),
        // 3d/glb/a-083-fachwerkhaus-gross.glb — 8.30 x 11.50 x 9.90 m.
        "house_large":    (source: Gltf("3d/glb/a-083-fachwerkhaus-gross.glb"), scale: 1.0, attribution: None, animations: {}),

        // ---- the fallen ring: eight ruins and six rubble mounds
        //
        // `docs/gameplay/world.md`: *"the war is already lost … **Ashgate has long since
        // fallen**; the Vanguard runs **salvage missions into its own ruins**"* — and until
        // 2026-08-19 the district built for that premise was an intact market town with a
        // full roofscape. The user, 2026-08-18: *„das ist nicht die echte map!"*.
        //
        // These fourteen rows are the kit that answers it. They are worn by
        // `world::map::plan_blocks` exactly the way the three house rows above are worn —
        // `BlockPlan.model`, one name per box — and which house wears which is decided by
        // `maps.ron: layout.damage`, not here.
        //
        // ⚠️ **The numbers in the comments are measured, not read off a spec sheet**: the
        // full extent of each file's own `hit.min`/`hit.max` pair, x / y / z in metres, and
        // `tests/world.rs::f003_the_ruin_catalogue_is_what_the_glb_files_really_measure`
        // parses all fourteen files and falls over on a re-export that moves them. ⚠️ The
        // pair is a CORNER pair and not a min/max pair — `hit.max.z < hit.min.z` on all 278
        // files of the drop — so the extent is taken with `abs`.
        //
        // ✅ Every one of them carries `hook.*` empties (1 to 3 each, `hook.bruchkante` —
        // "break edge" — on four of them), so the artist had a rope in mind. They are
        // anchorable for the same reason every other surface in Ashgate is: *„überall! ohne
        // ausnahmen!"*.

        // 3d/glb/a-089-ruine-dach-eingestuerzt.glb — 7.04 x 2.62 x 5.16 m.
        "ruin_roof_collapsed": (source: Gltf("3d/glb/a-089-ruine-dach-eingestuerzt.glb"), scale: 1.0, attribution: None, animations: {}),
        // 3d/glb/a-089-ruine-dach-haelfte.glb — 6.72 x 4.74 x 4.93 m.
        "ruin_roof_half":      (source: Gltf("3d/glb/a-089-ruine-dach-haelfte.glb"), scale: 1.0, attribution: None, animations: {}),
        // 3d/glb/a-089-ruine-giebel.glb — 6.47 x 8.49 x 4.01 m. The tallest of the kit: a
        // gable wall still standing over a house that is not there any more.
        "ruin_gable":          (source: Gltf("3d/glb/a-089-ruine-giebel.glb"), scale: 1.0, attribution: None, animations: {}),
        // 3d/glb/a-089-ruine-haufen.glb — 7.49 x 2.40 x 5.81 m.
        "ruin_heap":           (source: Gltf("3d/glb/a-089-ruine-haufen.glb"), scale: 1.0, attribution: None, animations: {}),
        // 3d/glb/a-089-ruine-obergeschoss.glb — 6.95 x 5.55 x 4.93 m.
        "ruin_upper_floor":    (source: Gltf("3d/glb/a-089-ruine-obergeschoss.glb"), scale: 1.0, attribution: None, animations: {}),
        // 3d/glb/a-089-ruine-pfeiler.glb — 5.86 x 9.00 x 4.87 m.
        "ruin_pillar":         (source: Gltf("3d/glb/a-089-ruine-pfeiler.glb"), scale: 1.0, attribution: None, animations: {}),
        // 3d/glb/a-089-ruine-wand-ecke.glb — 6.47 x 5.60 x 6.80 m.
        "ruin_wall_corner":    (source: Gltf("3d/glb/a-089-ruine-wand-ecke.glb"), scale: 1.0, attribution: None, animations: {}),
        // 3d/glb/a-089-ruine-wand-hoch.glb — 6.22 x 6.94 x 3.96 m.
        "ruin_wall_high":      (source: Gltf("3d/glb/a-089-ruine-wand-hoch.glb"), scale: 1.0, attribution: None, animations: {}),

        // The mounds. Nothing in this group is over 3 m authored, and that is the whole point
        // of it: rubble takes the **ground** away and leaves the air alone (see
        // `maps.ron: layout.damage.spill_m`).
        //
        // 3d/glb/a-090-schutt-balken.glb — 4.10 x 2.10 x 3.70 m.
        "rubble_beams":        (source: Gltf("3d/glb/a-090-schutt-balken.glb"), scale: 1.0, attribution: None, animations: {}),
        // 3d/glb/a-090-schutt-deckung.glb — 3.70 x 1.20 x 3.31 m.
        "rubble_cover":        (source: Gltf("3d/glb/a-090-schutt-deckung.glb"), scale: 1.0, attribution: None, animations: {}),
        // 3d/glb/a-090-schutt-flach.glb — 3.94 x 0.90 x 2.95 m.
        "rubble_flat":         (source: Gltf("3d/glb/a-090-schutt-flach.glb"), scale: 1.0, attribution: None, animations: {}),
        // 3d/glb/a-090-schutt-haufen-gross.glb — 6.20 x 3.00 x 4.80 m.
        "rubble_heap_large":   (source: Gltf("3d/glb/a-090-schutt-haufen-gross.glb"), scale: 1.0, attribution: None, animations: {}),
        // 3d/glb/a-090-schutt-hoch.glb — 4.20 x 1.80 x 3.50 m.
        "rubble_high":         (source: Gltf("3d/glb/a-090-schutt-hoch.glb"), scale: 1.0, attribution: None, animations: {}),
        // 3d/glb/a-090-schutt-wandstueck.glb — 4.33 x 2.40 x 2.80 m.
        "rubble_wall_piece":   (source: Gltf("3d/glb/a-090-schutt-wandstueck.glb"), scale: 1.0, attribution: None, animations: {}),

        // WOULD BE: nothing in the drop. `scale.ron heights_m.tree` is 12.0 and `maps.ron`
        // draws a 2.5 x 12.0 x 2.5 block; the drop's smallest giant tree
        // (`a-105-riesenbaum-gerade`) is 34 m and the tallest 48 m. Scaling a 48 m tree by
        // 0.25 shrinks its hook points and its trunk into a different asset.
        // BLOCKER: **a missing model, not a scale factor** — and the same `ModelName`-on-a-
        // block hop as the houses.
        "tree_giant":     (source: Primitive, scale: 1.0, attribution: None, animations: {}),

        // WOULD BE: 3d/glb/a-130-nachschubstation.glb — 3.90 x 4.20 x 3.35 m.
        // BLOCKER: `missions.ron hub.refuel_stations` is a bare point today and `maps.ron`
        // draws the racks as two hand-tuned blocks (docs/FINDINGS.md FIND-080), so binding
        // this changes a silhouette that was measured on purpose and needs its own
        // before/after picture — plus the same `ModelName`-on-a-block hop.
        "crate_resupply": (source: Primitive, scale: 1.0, attribution: None, animations: {}),
    },
    // How far a model's own `cortex` empty may sit from the height scale.ron names for that
    // size class before the loader shouts. 0.15 m is a fifth of a stone course — visible in
    // the silhouette, and far below the smallest head (0.42 m on the small titan).
    cortex_tolerance_m: 0.15,

    // -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------
    // lighting — the sun, the sky and the fog. **The one thing every number here exists
    // for: a roof, a wall in the sun and a wall in the shade have to be three different
    // values.** Measured before this block existed (docs/FINDINGS.md FIND-071): a vertical
    // wall face read luminance 183.2 and the ground beside it 183.3 — the same value, on
    // two surfaces at right angles to each other, because with `illuminance 10 000` against
    // the default exposure (ev100 9.7) **every face with NdotL > 0.73 clipped to white**.
    // That is what "alles sehr flat" is, and it was arithmetic, not taste.
    //
    // No hue is invented here: the palette stays `maps.ron: palette`, and none of the three
    // signal colours (cyan/amber/crimson) appears — the sky is slate and haze, the shadow
    // fill is sky-blue bounce. This block is light and depth, nothing else
    // (docs/gameplay/world.md, "Visual style").
    lighting: (
        sun: (
            // Linear RGB, and warm only just — the tone is elegiac, not golden hour
            // (docs/gameplay/world.md). It is a *tint*, not one of the signal colours.
            color: (1.0, 0.945, 0.86),

            // lux. 52 000 is a hazy-bright day (clear noon sun is ~100 000, overcast
            // ~10 000 — which is what the old value was, and it was a *ceiling* against the
            // exposure below, not a brightness).
            //
            // Chosen so that a stone_gray face (albedo 0.42) with NdotL = 1 lands at
            //   0.42/pi * 52000 * exposure(13.0) = 0.72 linear
            // — bright, and still under the clip.
            illuminance_lux: 52000.0,

            // Where the sun stands. **Compass degrees, our yaw convention**: 0 = -Z,
            // +90 = +X (docs/conventions.md), and elevation is degrees above the horizon.
            //
            // 108 / 36 is not decoration, it is the whole separation. It puts the sun at
            //   to_sun = (0.769, 0.588, 0.250)
            // so the four faces of a box get four different NdotL:
            //   +X wall 0.769 · roof 0.588 · +Z wall 0.250 · -X and -Z walls 0.0 (fill only)
            // A sun straight overhead gives a bright roof and four identical walls; a sun on
            // the diagonal gives two bright walls and two dark ones. This gives four steps.
            azimuth_deg: 108.0,
            // Low enough that a 11 m house throws a 15 m shadow across a 7 m street, high
            // enough that the street is not one solid black slot: tan(36 deg) = 0.727.
            elevation_deg: 36.0,

            // **The expensive switch** (docs/lessons/performance.md rule 5). Measured here,
            // with the number the lesson asked for and never had — FIND-071.
            shadows: true,
            // Shadow map edge in texels, per cascade. 2048 over a 4-cascade split holds the
            // first cascade (40 m) at ~2 cm/texel; 4096 doubles the memory for a district
            // that is boxes.
            shadow_map_size: 2048,
            // 4 cascades over 400 m: the district is 400 m across and the wall is 120 m, so
            // the shadow range has to cover a whole quarter, not the ten metres in front of
            // the nose — a flyer sees the roofscape, not a corridor.
            cascades: 4,
            first_cascade_far_bound_m: 40.0,
            shadow_distance_m: 400.0,
            cascade_overlap: 0.2,
            // Acne bias. Bevy's defaults (0.02 / 1.8) are tuned for a metre-scale scene; a
            // 400 m cascade stretches every texel, so both go up. Measured against the
            // street canyons, which are the worst case: 7 m wide, 11 m tall.
            shadow_depth_bias: 0.06,
            shadow_normal_bias: 2.4,
        ),

        // The fill light. **Cool on purpose**: the sun is warm, the fill is sky-blue, and
        // that warm/cool split is what makes an unlit face read as "in shadow" instead of
        // "dark grey". No hue invented — this is the sky's own colour bouncing.
        ambient: (
            color: (0.45, 0.55, 0.80),
            // cd/m^2. **The one number two tests squeeze from both ends**, and the window
            // between them is now half a stop wide:
            //
            //   stone_gray (0.43) must stay UNDER 20 % of a sunlit face, or an outdoor shadow
            //     stops being a shadow                            -> brightness <= 4630
            //     `f071_a_roof_a_sunlit_wall_and_a_shaded_wall_are_three_different_values`
            //   the LAMP over the aisle must stay at least 5x the fill on the same surface,
            //     or somebody has raised the world instead of lighting the room
            //                                                     -> brightness <= 3528
            //   and the fill must still lift `brick_red` to 5 % of that lamplit floor, or the
            //     face no lamp reaches is black                   -> brightness >= 1459
            //     both in `f019_a_roofed_room_lives_on_its_lamp_and_the_fill_is_the_second_term`
            //
            // 2400 sits in that window: stone_gray 10.4 % of the sun, the lamp 7.3x the fill,
            // brick_red 8.2 % of the lamplit floor.
            //
            // ## Why it came back DOWN from 4200 on 2026-08-13
            //
            // 4200 was put here the same morning as an explicit **mitigation** (FIND-078): the
            // hall had no light of its own, so the only lever on it was the world's fill, and
            // the fill was raised until the racks were 10.6 sRGB levels off the wall behind
            // them. That lever cost the exterior 1:1 — a shadowed wall rose with everything
            // else, and shadow-against-sun went 29.1 % -> 38.0 % in sRGB, which is the contrast
            // `FIND-071` was fought for.
            //
            // The hall has two lamps now (`maps.ron: ashgate.lights`), and they do what no
            // amount of fill could: they give a box a lit face and a shaded one. Four runs of
            // the same binary, same tick, same camera (FIND-080 §4):
            //
            //   4200 + lamps: rack 90.6  wall 141.7  floor 150.3  EXT shadow/sun 38.0 %
            //   2400 + lamps: rack 83.9  wall 137.8  floor 147.0  EXT shadow/sun 29.1 %
            //
            // **6.7 sRGB levels inside for the exterior's whole contrast back.** The interior
            // is no longer paid for out of the world, and that is the trade this line takes.
            brightness: 2400.0,
        ),

        // The sky is a dome, not a `ClearColor`. Three stops, linear RGB, vertically
        // interpolated: zenith -> horizon over the upper half, horizon -> nadir over the
        // lower. **The horizon stop is also the fog colour** (below), which is what makes a
        // distant block dissolve into the sky instead of into a grey wall.
        sky: (
            // Deep slate blue, and it is only ever seen at a steep look-up: the ramp is linear
            // in `sin(elevation)` (`render::light::dome_mesh`), so this stop arrives at 90 deg
            // and half of it at 30. It has to be an extreme, or the middle of the ramp — which
            // is the only part of the sky a flyer sees — is grey.
            zenith: (0.025, 0.058, 0.150),

            // **Pale warm haze, and it is now actually pale and actually warm.** It was
            // (0.115, 0.112, 0.104) until 2026-08-19: red minus blue 0.011, linear luminance
            // 0.112 — a mid grey that sat BELOW the district's own mid-tone (a stone_gray wall
            // at a grazing angle is 0.265). Measured on the frame (FIND-112), the whole band a
            // flyer looks through read rgb (90.9, 90.2, 88.4) at 2.8 % saturation with an
            // 11-level spread over 230 rows, and the 120 m wall in front of it read 107.6 —
            // **the sky was darker than the city standing against it**, so the wall had no
            // silhouette and the sky was the "flat grey" of the report.
            //
            // 0.470/0.412/0.335 is linear luminance 0.419 = **1.58x** that grazing wall. On a
            // 52 000 lux hazy-bright day (`sun.illuminance_lux`) the haze band IS about as
            // bright as a sunlit surface, and it stays under the brightest one (a sunlit
            // stone_gray face, 0.68), so the sun is still the brightest thing in the frame.
            // Measured on the standard vantage, same binary, same tick, only this block
            // different: sky 90.2 -> **149.5** and the 120 m wall in front of it 107.6 ->
            // 140.0, i.e. the sky went from 17 levels DARKER than the wall to 10 brighter, and
            // the wall has a silhouette for the first time.
            // Two brighter hazes were rendered and rejected: 0.500/0.440/0.360 with
            // `start_m 25` reached the same depth but took the near quarter's contrast with it
            // (distinct tones 22 156 -> 15 836; this one holds 19 769).
            // `tests/render.rs::f071_the_sky_carries_its_hue_in_the_band_you_actually_fly_in`
            // holds the warmth, the ramp and the silhouette from three sides.
            horizon: (0.470, 0.412, 0.335),

            // Below the horizon — the ground haze the district stands in. Held at ~0.45 of the
            // horizon and not at the old 0.26: the fog fades far geometry to the HORIZON stop,
            // so a nadir far below it draws a seam across the bottom of any frame that looks
            // down past the edge of the ground slab.
            nadir: (0.210, 0.184, 0.150),
            // Metres. The dome is pinned to the eye, so this only has to stay inside the
            // camera's far plane (1000 m, `PerspectiveProjection` default) with room for the
            // dome's own tessellation error. 900 would touch it on the diagonal.
            radius_m: 820.0,
            // Segments around / rings up. 32x16 is 512 quads for a surface that is a smooth
            // gradient: more rings buy nothing, fewer show banding at the horizon.
            segments: 32,
            rings: 16,
        ),

        // Distance fog — "aggressive", and it does double duty: atmosphere and culling
        // (docs/gameplay/world.md, "Lighting"). Linear falloff, because it is the only one
        // whose two numbers mean something you can walk out and measure.
        fog: (
            // = `sky.horizon`. They must match, or the horizon becomes a visible seam.
            color: (0.470, 0.412, 0.335),
            // Metres. The far side of the district is ~400 m from the vantage and the wall
            // crown ~450 m.
            //
            // ⚠️ This line used to read `120.0 / 780.0` and used to say "the wall sits at 50 %".
            // It did not: 450 m over 120..780 is **36 %**, and 300 m is 27 %. The paragraph was
            // reasoning about a range it did not have, and the arithmetic was never done — that
            // is the whole of why an "aggressive" fog measured 6 sRGB levels across the
            // district for a week. The 60/700 that "was too much" was never measured either;
            // what was measured is below.
            //
            // **`end_m` has to stay under `sky.radius_m`.** The dome is opaque and pinned to
            // the eye, so nothing beyond 820 m is ever visible: an `end` past it is a number
            // the fog can never reach, and the horizon then never quite becomes the sky.
            // 900 was in the file for one round and
            // `tests/render.rs::f071_the_sky_casts_no_shadow_and_the_fog_meets_it_at_the_horizon`
            // is what found it.
            // **60/470 since 2026-08-19, and the old 120/780 is why this block existed and
            // did nothing.** Linear falloff over 120..780 m puts 300 m — the far side of the
            // district — at 27 % of the way in, and against a fog colour that was darker than
            // the scene that bought 6 sRGB levels. Measured, ten identical gantry columns down
            // one 315 m lane, same face, same light (FIND-112):
            //
            //   17.8 m  131.3        119.8 m  131.7        224.3 m  132.5
            //   50.8 m  131.6        154.8 m  133.2        259.2 m  124.9
            //
            // Eight samples over 240 m inside 8 levels of each other. That is not aggressive
            // fog, that is no fog, and `docs/gameplay/world.md` asks for the opposite.
            //
            // 60/470: the whole street in front of the player is untouched (`start_m` is one
            // gantry bay plus a half), 300 m is **58 %** in and 450 m — the wall crown from the
            // standard vantage — is **95 %**, so the wall reads as a distance. `start_m` is not
            // 25: at 25 the haze already sat on the near quarter from a 62 m vantage and the
            // frame lost a sixth of its tones for depth it did not need there.
            // The same series, same binary, only this block different:
            //
            //   17.8 m  131.3        119.8 m  135.9        224.3 m  146.6
            //   50.8 m  131.6        154.8 m  140.6        259.2 m  143.9
            //
            // monotone, and +12.6 levels where it was -6.4. A wall in SHADE moves further,
            // because the haze has more room over it: 51.5 -> 69.2 over the first 87 m.
            // `end_m` still has to stay under `sky.radius_m` (820) and
            // `tests/render.rs::f071_the_sky_casts_no_shadow_and_the_fog_meets_it_at_the_horizon`
            // is what checks it.
            start_m: 60.0,
            end_m: 470.0,
        ),

        // ev100. 12.85 sits between Bevy's OVERCAST (12.0) and SUNLIGHT (15.0) and is the
        // number the two blocks above are solved against — moving it moves everything.
        // Bevy's own default is BLENDER (9.7), which is 10x more exposure than this and is
        // the direct cause of the clipping measured in FIND-071.
        exposure_ev100: 12.85,
    ),
)
